uests and processing BFD (Bidirectional Forwarding Detection) packets, while acting as a centralized management component responsible for data synchronization, network traffic processing, and enforcing security policies such as user authentication and login controls. The BMC interfaces with consumer modules and other system components via register operations and supports software configuration through the CSR configuration dictionary, where it is treated as a class for software management.\n\nThe BMC is a critical component in observability architectures, serving as a source of telemetry data transmitted to the OpenTelemetry Collector via the OTLP (OpenTelemetry Protocol) over a unidirectional channel by default, with support for secure communication enhanced by two-factor authentication requiring prior import of both root and client certificates. It is involved in the configuration and upgrade of the White Label Package and is associated with running specific firmware, such as openUBMC firmware, which enables system monitoring and management capabilities. While fundamentally a hardware-based microcontroller, the BMC serves as a critical coordinator between firmware, software, and system components, ensuring the operability, security, and manageability of server platforms across their lifecycle. Some descriptions also indicate support for firmware updates via JTAG and execution of VME (VersaModule Eurocard) file operations, highlighting its role in low-level system control and configuration.<SEP>BMC is a system component that supports CPLD upgrades through SVF file parsing and JTAG signal control.<SEP>BMC (Baseboard Management Controller) is a specialized microcontroller used for monitoring and managing system health and firmware upgrades.<SEP>BMC is a Baseboard Management Controller responsible for monitoring and managing system health and network functions.<SEP>BMC (Baseboard Management Controller) is a dedicated controller used for monitoring and managing server 